# Security Rules — Non-Negotiable

The previous version of this app shipped `timesheet_adjustments` with Row Level
Security DISABLED, which meant anyone holding the anon/publishable key could read
or write every row in that table with no login required. This must never happen
again in this rebuild.

## Row Level Security (RLS)

- Every table created in the `public` schema MUST have RLS enabled in the same
  migration that creates the table. Never create a table and defer enabling RLS
  to "later."
- Every table must have at least one policy before it is considered done. A table
  with RLS enabled but zero policies is effectively locked to everyone except
  `service_role` — confirm this is the intended state if no policy is added yet,
  don't leave it ambiguous.
- Default posture: no anon-key read or write access to any table containing
  worker data, timesheet data, salary data, or client billing data. Only
  authenticated, role-scoped access is allowed.
- Before generating any migration that creates a table, explicitly state in your
  response which RLS policy you are applying and why. If you are not enabling RLS
  on a new public table, you must say so explicitly and explain why — never do
  this silently.

## Audit trail

- Every write (insert/update/delete) to `timesheets`, `salary_sheets`,
  `client_payouts`, or `workers` must be captured in `audit_log` with:
  `user_id`, `table_name`, `record_id`, `action`, `old_value`, `new_value`,
  `changed_at`.
- `timesheet_adjustments` is the audit trail for retroactive payroll corrections
  specifically. It must always have RLS enabled, since it is load-bearing for
  the payroll history — treat it with the same care as the `timesheets` table
  itself, not as a secondary/logging table.

## Verification habit

After any migration that adds or modifies tables, proactively suggest running
Supabase's advisor check for security issues (RLS gaps, missing policies) before
considering the migration complete. Do not wait to be asked.

## Secrets

- Never hardcode Supabase URLs, service_role keys, or any API secrets directly in
  application code. All secrets must come from environment variables, and
  `.env` must remain in `.gitignore` at all times — never commit it.
- The `service_role` key must never be used in client-side/browser code. It is
  server-side only (edge functions, backend scripts).